    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#chandragupta-maurya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya.jpg" alt="Chandragupta Maurya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chandragupt_maurya_Birla_mandir_6_dec_2009_(31)_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">आशीष भटनागर at Hindi Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maurya Empire was founded around 321 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, who overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magadha">Magadha</a> and exploited the political vacuum left in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s campaigns. Guided, according to tradition, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brahmins">Brahmin</a> strategist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chanakya">Chanakya</a> (also known as Kautilya), Chandragupta built a vast realm stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> to the north-western frontier. In a war and treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> around 305–303 BCE, he gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arachosia">Arachosia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gedrosia">Gedrosia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paropamisadae">Paropamisadae</a>, covering much of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pakistan">Pakistan</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, in return for 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">war elephants</a>, and received the Greek envoy Megasthenes at his court. The empire's rise was facilitated by the weakness of rival dynasties, the resources of the Gangetic heartland, and a centralised administrative vision rooted in earlier Magadhan statecraft. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#patna">Patna</a>), already the Nanda capital, served as the imperial centre, which Megasthenes described as a vast city ringed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a> palisade. Chandragupta's consolidation laid the foundation for one of the largest empires in Indian history, capable of projecting military and economic power across diverse ecological and cultural zones.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pataliputra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pataliputra.jpg" alt="Pataliputra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pataliputra">Pataliputra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pataliputra6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pyule, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maurya Empire developed a highly structured economy anchored in agriculture, trade and state-controlled revenue. Society was hierarchical, with the emperor and his court at the apex, followed by officials, priests, merchants, artisans and a large peasantry; Megasthenes famously divided Indian society into seven classes, with farmers the most numerous. Agriculture formed the backbone of the economy, relying on the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges</a> basin and on irrigation works such as the Sudarshana reservoir in Saurashtra, attributed to Chandragupta's governor, to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a>, wheat, barley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pulses">pulses</a>. Trade flourished internally and externally, linking the subcontinent with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a> and the Hellenistic world along routes such as the great northern road (Uttarapatha) and the river systems. The state levied land revenue, trade duties and taxes on artisans, and directly operated mines, forests and workshops, supporting a large army and bureaucracy. Urban centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taxila">Taxila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ujjain">Ujjain</a> thrived on craft production, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> punch-marked coinage and long-distance commerce. This combination of agriculture, state enterprise and trade underpinned the empire's stability and its roads and public works.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#kalinga-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalinga-war.jpg" alt="Kalinga War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#kalinga-war">Kalinga War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chandragupta_Maurya_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jagged 85 at English Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maurya army was a large, highly organised and professional force. It comprised the four traditional arms of infantry, cavalry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">war elephants</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chariot">chariots</a>, and Greek sources report that it was administered by a board of thirty officials divided into six committees, including ones for the navy and for supply. War elephants provided both intimidation and battlefield dominance, while cavalry ensured mobility for reconnaissance and rapid strikes. River fleets protected trade and supported operations along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and its tributaries. Chandragupta and his successors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bindusara">Bindusara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashoka">Ashoka</a>, conducted campaigns that expanded and secured the empire, which eventually covered almost the whole subcontinent except the far south. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kalinga-war">Kalinga War</a>, Ashoka's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kalinga">Kalinga</a> around 261 BCE, which by his own account caused a hundred thousand deaths and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deportation">deportation</a> of many more, was the empire's last major war and led him to renounce conquest by force. Military discipline and the fortification of key cities enabled the Mauryas to control a vast and diverse territory, and the army served both to defend the frontiers and to enforce administrative authority within them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#edicts-of-ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka.jpg" alt="Edicts of Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka_Lauriya_Areraj_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sachin kumar tiwary, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maurya Empire developed one of the earliest and most comprehensive administrative frameworks in South Asian history. Government was centralised under the emperor, assisted by a council of ministers, while major provinces centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taxila">Taxila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ujjain">Ujjain</a>, Tosali and Suvarnagiri were governed by royal princes (kumaras) as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a>, supported by senior officials known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahamatra">Mahamatras</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arthashastra">Arthashastra</a>, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chanakya">Chanakya</a> but probably compiled and revised over several centuries, gives detailed guidance on administration, espionage, law and diplomacy, and is often used to illuminate Mauryan practice. Law combined royal regulation with customary practice, covering property, crime and social obligations. Specialised officers oversaw agriculture, trade, taxation and justice, enabling coordinated government across diverse provinces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s reign added a distinctive dimension, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a> inscribed on rocks and pillars proclaiming dhamma, welfare measures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#well">wells</a>, rest-houses and medical care for people and animals, and special officers (dhamma-mahamattas) to promote moral conduct. This administrative sophistication enabled effective coordination across a vast empire, balancing centralised control with adaptability to local conditions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maurya Empire had a profound influence across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-asia">South Asia</a> and beyond, establishing a model of centralised imperial government that shaped later Indian states. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashoka">Ashoka</a>, royal patronage helped spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buddhism">Buddhism</a> throughout the subcontinent and beyond; his son Mahinda is traditionally credited with bringing the faith to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and missions are said to have reached Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The Mauryas promoted trade along overland routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a> and maritime corridors across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, encouraging the exchange of goods, ideas and technologies. Ashoka's thirteenth rock edict names the Hellenistic kings Antiochus II, Ptolemy II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> Gonatas, Magas of Cyrene and Alexander of Epirus as recipients of his dhamma missions, highlighting the empire's engagement with the wider world. Its roads, stupas and polished sandstone pillars, among them the Lion Capital of Sarnath, which became the national emblem of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, left a lasting artistic and architectural legacy. The Maurya legacy in administration, economic regulation and statecraft influenced successor empires such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gupta-empire">Guptas</a> and left enduring cultural and religious imprints across the subcontinent and beyond.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Maurya Empire declined rapidly after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s death around 232 BCE under a combination of political, economic and regional pressures. Succession disputes and weaker rulers undermined central authority, and the empire may have been divided between rival branches of the dynasty. Maintaining a large bureaucracy and army strained the treasury, particularly as revenues fluctuated. Peripheral regions, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kalinga">Kalinga</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a> and the north-west, increasingly asserted their independence, and Greco-Bactrian rulers began to push into the north-west in the early second century BCE. Around 185 BCE the last Maurya emperor, Brihadratha, was assassinated by his commander-in-chief, Pushyamitra Shunga, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shunga-empire">Shunga dynasty</a> in the Gangetic heartland, marking the end of Mauryan rule. Economic contraction, internal factionalism and the difficulty of sustaining imperial infrastructure compounded the decline. Mauryan administrative practice, artistic traditions and Buddhist patronage nevertheless persisted, influencing successor states and regional institutions for centuries.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Maurya Empire stands as one of the largest empires in Indian history, whose centralised administration, guided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arthashastra">Arthashastra</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s promotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dharma">dharma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buddhism">Buddhism</a> left a model of governance and a religious legacy that long outlived the dynasty.</em></p>

